    "message": "feat(desktop): give mcc-desktop a real window, and make its lifecycle honest\n\nmcc-desktop was a tray icon whose only way to show the dashboard was\nwebbrowser.open() -- a tab dropped into whatever browser was default, among\nevery other tab already open. There was no window belonging to this product.\n\n\"Just embed a webview\" is three answers wearing one name: WebView2 on Windows,\nWKWebView on macOS, WebKitGTK on Linux. They are not interchangeable, and\npywebview on Linux needs apt-installed system packages our wheel-only\ninstallers cannot provide.\n\nAn audit of admin.js settled the order. Three things the dashboard depends on\nbreak inside an embedded webview:\n\n    window.open(_blank)   admin.js:2302, :2364   BOTH OAuth logins\n    <a download>          admin.js:7649          the analytics export window\n    navigator.clipboard   admin.js:3601, :7906   every copy button\n\nThe first is disqualifying on its own -- a window where login silently does\nnothing is worse than a browser tab. So the window is a seam with an ordered\nchain (app-mode -> pywebview -> browser tab), preferring Chromium app-mode\nbecause it IS a browser and therefore has zero functional regressions. The\npreference persists as `window` in desktop.json and is pinnable; an\nunavailable pin degrades with a warning rather than raising, because a\nguardrail must degrade and not become an outage. pywebview ships unavailable\nby default -- it is not a dependency, and it cannot guarantee the download and\nexternal-link settings it would need -- so the delivered chain is app-mode ->\ntab. ADR 0002 records the reasoning, including why Tauri was rejected on three\nindependent grounds.\n\nSix lifecycle cases now behave:\n\n- A second launch rings the running instance's doorbell and exits, instead of\n  opening another browser tab. Backed by a file rather than a socket: binding\n  a listener raises the Windows firewall prompt, and a port can be taken by the\n  very program the app is already fighting over. The lock owner clears the file\n  before watching, so a signal from an instance that died is discarded.\n- The port probe distinguishes three states, not two. preflight_proxy's \"no\"\n  covered both \"nothing is listening\" and \"something that is not us is\", and\n  spawning into the second produced a bind failure with no explanation. The\n  message now names the process and pid holding the port.\n- A dead server notifies only after three consecutive failed probes, so a\n  self-update replacing its own process is not reported as death.\n- Headless refuses with the working alternative rather than the missing\n  display, quoting the CONFIGURED admin URL -- a message naming :8082 on a\n  machine serving :9000 sends the reader somewhere empty.\n- Closing the window no longer stops the server. minimize_to_tray was\n  persisted and echoed by --status since 5.19.0 and consumed by nothing; it\n  works for the first time here.\n- The tray reads tray_icon_bytes() rather than the app icon. 5.26.0 shipped\n  that tighter render for exactly this surface and nothing called it.\n\nadmin_routes' update_desktop reconstructed DesktopState from the payload, so\nwithout carrying `window` through, every unrelated dashboard save silently\nreset the preference.\n\nVerification:\n\n- Full suite: 6 failed, 4283 passed, 26 skipped. Baseline on unmodified\n  fork/main in the same shell with ENABLE_WEB_SERVER_TOOLS/EXA_API_KEY\n  neutralised: 6 failed, 4214 passed. Same six PowerShell installer failures,\n  +69 tests, zero new failures.\n- ruff format, ruff check, ty: clean. No suppressions; the one ty error found\n  (indexing Pillow's flattened-data, typed as a flat int sequence) was fixed by\n  reading the alpha band instead.\n- Guards mutation-tested and confirmed to fail BY NAME: chain ordering, stale\n  activation file, the consecutive-failure threshold, the tray icon variant,\n  and the configured-port refusal.\n\nNot verified: no live window was displayed. App-mode command construction is\nunit-tested, not executed against a real browser, and the macOS/Linux binary\nsearches are monkeypatched. The pywebview provider is entirely unexercised\ncode, which is why it ships gated off rather than merely untested.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
